    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e9e863f0-8dc5-502c-a8f4-62212d388528",
      ":question/problem": "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^{2}}{36}+\\frac{y^{2}}{64}=1.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{36}+\\frac{y^{2}}{64}=1$ in the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{36}+\\frac{y^2}{64}&=\\frac{(6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{36}+\\frac{(8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{64}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is $8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
