      ":question/problem": "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y)=⟨11 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^{2}}{121}+\\frac{z^{2}}{9}=1.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{121}+\\frac{z^{2}}{9}=1$ in the $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨11 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{121}+\\frac{z^2}{9}&=\\frac{(11 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{121}+\\frac{(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{9}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨11 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y,\\,3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $y\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is $3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$.",
